<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.9.8: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00530</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.9.8: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>75ab607a-8a54-4582-84fc-41e481ea8bf0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="6a5dfa57-fef9-41e5-b8cd-c8e299fa4c13"><problem><para>
      <para>1. Here is an inequality: \(\frac{7x+6}2\leq3x+2\)Select <emphasis>two</emphasis> of the values that are a solution to the inequality.</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>["\\(x = -3\\)", "\\(x = -2\\)"]</para><para>
      <para>The correct answers are \(x = -3\), \(x = -2\).
      </para>
      <para>Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(20\gt 15\)
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section>
<para>For questions 3 – 4, use the following information:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>Scooter rental company A charges $9 per hour plus a $11 initiation fee. Scooter rental company B charges $15 per hour.</para>
</blockquote>

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="eadc8e80-78e7-4b3a-bf8a-ae7a81f3a6b4"><problem><para>
      <para>3. A customer writes the following inequality to represent the situation: \(9h + 11 &gt; 15h\).</para>
      <para>Which of the following is true about the given inequality?</para>
    </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>Company A costs more than Company B.</para><para>Company A costs less than Company B.</para><para>Company A costs the same as Company B.</para><para>h represents the number of scooters rented.</para></problem><solution><para>Company A costs more than Company B.</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is Company A costs more than Company B. </para>
      <para>Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(20 \lt 5\)
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="bc34b533-547f-47a7-942e-01f28d487514"><problem><para>
      <para>6. Which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint you found in the previous question?</para>
    </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>Cycle Rentals</para><para>Bikes Unlimited</para></problem><solution><para>Cycle Rentals</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is Cycle Rentals.
      </para>
      <para>
        The constraint from question 5 is \(x=4\). 
        Since a number larger than 4 is needed to determine which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint, choose to substitute in the number 5 for \(x\) to calculate how many membership points can be earned in 5 days.
        Cycle Rentals: \(20x=20(5)=100\) membership points
        Bikes Unlimited: \(5x+60=5(5)+60=25+60=85\) membership points 
      </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="90b0f163-6e68-4323-abe1-441b5d71512c"><problem><para>
      <para>7. Which number line represents the solution to the inequality, \(20x &gt; 5x + 60\), representing the situation when you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals?</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9baf590bad3d5c61a1f40792b71b0ba0e6e5f71b"/></media></figure></para>
        <para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with a closed circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8006903b8bee6446e2bf991eedd28c997ef3d0f4"/></media></figure></para>
        <para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with a closed circle on the point 4. The number line to the left of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2cdbc8eae1d8ce8f1f626cbccc9d0285c406b8a1"/></media></figure></para>
        <para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the left of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d320204ee9b51df65b814e8ddcbb82312b7809ad"/></media></figure></para>
      </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>A.</para><para>B.</para><para>C.</para><para>D.</para></problem><solution><para>A.</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is<figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9baf590bad3d5c61a1f40792b71b0ba0e6e5f71b"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(20x \gt 5x+60\) 
        \(15x \gt 60 \)
        \(x \gt 4\) 
        The graph will have an open circle at 4, since the solution can not be equal to 4, with the arrow pointing to infinity to the right because the solution is greater than 4.
      </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
